# perf_mem2_2026-09-26 — memset/memcpy round 2: instrumentation findings (PLAN Phase 5 item 7)

Host: i265 (hybrid Core Ultra 7 265K). Tool: callgrind instruction count
(`valgrind --tool=callgrind --dump-instr=yes`) on the heapdbg build
(`CARGO_PROFILE_RELEASE_DEBUG=line-tables-only CARGO_TARGET_DIR=target/heapdbg`).

Cell: `perf_encode gradient 1024 1024 40 10 <prefix> 0` (still, p10).
Baseline at main 54e353b5: **300,114,657 Ir total; memset 14,326,072;
memcpy 10,480,775.**

This session landed no code change; it re-derived the round-1 diagnoses with
two ad-hoc probes (a per-(type,len,n,cap) counter inside `grown_out`, and a
per-class hit/miss counter inside `vecpool`'s `take_pooled`/`give_pooled`).
The probes were reverted; the numbers below are what they printed.

## grown_out — the diagnosis was wrong in detail, right in total

All 10,276 `buf.resize` calls fired with `len == 0` — FRESH allocations on
pool MISSES, not growth deltas of short parked buffers:

    i32: n=16 x768, n=64 x2816, n=256 x2064, n=1024 x640   (6,288 fills)
    u8 : n=16 x768, n=64 x2272, n=256 x823, n=1024 x124,
         n=4096 x1                                        (3,988 fills)

Pool hit/miss per class (takes): i32 misses 6,288 of 6,792 (~7% hit);
u8 ~78% hit. i16+u16 classes ~2,080 takes, ZERO hits.

Why the pool starves:
- `LeafEval::into_choice` moves `txb_q`/`u_q`/`v_q`/`u_recon`/`v_recon`
  through `PoolVec::into_vec` into the committed `LeafChoice` — the buffers
  leave the pool PERMANENTLY (dropped as plain `Vec`s when the partition
  trees die at frame end). ~6.3K i32 buffers lost ≈ the miss count.
- Live `Cand`s additionally hoard their `txb_q`/recon buffers for the whole
  leaf evaluation.
- `LeafEval::psq_resid`/`psq_resid10` (`Vec<i16>`/`Vec<i32>`) are built by
  `dirty_pool::<i16>(w*h)` then `.into_vec()` (mod.rs:1136/1220,
  light.rs:863) — the same permanent drain, which is why the i16 pool never
  hits once.

Consequence: for `grown_out` the memset is the INIT of a fresh allocation,
structural under the current ownership shape — each committed txb needs its
OWN live buffer, so misses are inherent. The two fix shapes that remain:

  1. Keep `psq_resid`/`psq_resid10` (and ideally the committed qcoeff/recon
     chain through `LeafChoice`/`BlockDecision`) as `PoolVec` so drops
     re-park the buffer. `psq_resid` alone is cheap to flip (LeafEval is a
     `Clone`d struct of pool buffers already; readers go through
     `ev.psq_resid()` -> `&[i16]`, which derefs cleanly). Estimated saving:
     the whole `dirty_pool::<i16>` memset (~642K Ir on this cell) plus
     i16/u16 pool warmup. Committed-path buffers only help ACROSS the point
     where their trees drop (frame end), so on a single-frame cell the
     into_vec chain buys ~nothing here — but it also costs ~nothing.
  2. The `extend`/`push` restructure the brief floats is likely NET-NEGATIVE:
     the quantizer writes ~1.88M elements through indexed stores; a push
     loop costs ~2-3 extra instructions per element against a memset's
     ~0.2 — worse than the ~2.9M Ir the fill costs.

## calloc-internal memset — 2,598,239 Ir, 100 calls

heaptrack on the same cell: 110 `alloc_zeroed` calls inside
`encode_tile_rows`'s per-tile closure (2.55M peak), dominated by per-tile
zeroed construction — `EntropyCtx::new` (~18 vecs: the palette colour
arrays and `NeighborMi` rows are the big ones) ×16 tiles, `try_vec![128u8;
ext_cbuf]` chroma recon canvases (262 KB each ×2 per tile — counted under
the caller's own memset, not calloc), `DeblockGeom::new` x2 (~524K Ir).

Fix shape (C-faithful): hoist `EntropyCtx` out of `walk_driver`'s per-tile
loop and add a `reset_for_tile()` that restores exactly what C's
`entropy_coding_reset_neighbor_arrays` + `reset_entropy_coding_picture`
reset (ec_process.c:60-117): partition context, luma + cb/cr coeff
neighbour arrays, txfm context (+ the port's inter_bw/inter_bh twins),
`prev_qindex`/`prev_delta_lf` scalars, and the tile bounds
(`tile_top_px`/`tile_left_px`/`tile_mi`). The mode/skip/palette/nmi arrays
are availability-gated and C does NOT reset them — leaving them warm is
C-faithful, not a shortcut. `fun_ectx`/`sim_ectx` in `tile_body` are
per-tile on worker threads: reuse there needs a per-thread slot and only
pays when tiles/thread > 1 (on this 20-thread box 16 tiles => 1 each — no
gain on this cell).

## memcpy — 10,480,775 Ir, mostly struct moves (unchanged from round 1)

Callers: `eval_candidate` 1.37M (98,880 calls), `commit_leaf` 968K
(80,896), `encode_block_syntax` 789K (46,656), `predict_unit` 651K
(10,400), `tx_unit_inner` 589K (44,544), `evaluate_leaf` ~774K combined,
`WarpRefineBlock` copy under evaluate_leaf 285K (2,080), `into_choice`
185K (2,080), `decide_leaf` 235K (2,080).

Named sites: `let cfg = frame.cfg` copies the ~200B `FunnelCfg` by value at
~7 call sites (inject.rs:206, mds1.rs:47/459/587, mds3.rs:62/209/506,
mod.rs:698); `eval_candidate` destructures `*g` (LeafGeom) and `*m`
(Mds3Ctx) by value per call; `warp_refine.rs:198` rebuilds a
`WarpRefineBlock` (~1.6 KB inline `samples: [WarpSamples; 8]` plus two
Vecs) per call. Fix: destructure by reference (`let LeafGeom{..} = g` on
`&LeafGeom` binds refs), `&FunnelCfg` params, and either pass
`WarpRefineBlock` by ref or box the `samples` member.
